\title{Mod-Exponential Windows Are Smoothness-Blind\\[4pt]
\large A structure theorem for cyclic orbit prefixes, an information bound,\\
and an exact no-free-lunch theorem for smoothness classification}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{14 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $N$ be an odd modulus and $a$ a base coprime to $N$. The mod-exponential sequence
$s_x = a^x \bmod N$ is the computational substrate of Pollard's $p-1$ factoring method,
which succeeds precisely when $N$ has a prime factor $p$ with $p-1$ dividing the
method's exponent $M = \lcmop(1,\dots,B)$. We ask whether this smoothness class --- a
genuine, dramatic, algorithmically exploitable weakness --- leaves any trace in the
statistics of a short window $s_0,\dots,s_{m-1}$ with $m \ll B$, and prove that it does
not, in the strongest available sense. We show that the entire collision structure of
the sequence is exactly the residue map $x \mapsto x \bmod d$ with $d = \ord_N(a)$, so
that a length-$m$ window depends on the instance only through $\min(m,d)$; that at most
$m+1$ distinct length-$m$ collision patterns exist across all bases and moduli, whence
a window carries at most $\log_2(m+1)$ bits; and that consequently, for every window
length $m$, there is an infinite family of odd moduli containing both smoothness classes
on which every real-valued statistic of the window attains $\AUC = 1/2$ exactly, under
every labelling. On the explicit matched pair $N_{\mathrm{smooth}} = 1009\cdot 1019$ and
$N_{\mathrm{general}} = 1019 \cdot 1039$ with $M = \lcmop(1,\dots,20)$, the $p-1$ method
returns a proper nontrivial divisor of the first and the trivial gcd on the second,
while the two length-$256$ windows are identical as combinatorial objects. The exact
criterion $r \mid a^M - 1 \iff \ord_r(a) \mid M$ localizes the discriminating
information to a single bit whose only known evaluation route is the $p-1$ computation
itself. We further prove a value-level invariance over a full period and show that all
results are instances of a statement about orbit prefixes of finite-order elements of
arbitrary monoids.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Multiplicative order; Pollard $p-1$ method; smooth numbers;
integer factorization; cyclic orbit prefix; ROC/AUC; no-free-lunch theorem; information
bound.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Integer factorization has resisted general attack for centuries, but individual
instances are not uniform in difficulty. Certain semiprimes $N = pq$ possess
\emph{structural weaknesses} that reduce factoring to a short computation. The oldest
and cleanest is smoothness of $p-1$, exploited by Pollard's $p-1$ method and, in
Lenstra's elliptic-curve generalization, by the smoothness of the order of a random
curve group.

Because a weak instance can be broken in milliseconds and a strong one resists
indefinitely, there is standing interest in a \emph{cheap screen}: a statistic
computable from $N$, or from a short computation involving $N$, that predicts weakness
at asymptotically lower cost than running the attack. Such a screen would matter
operationally (keyring auditing, target triage) and theoretically, since it would
constitute a nontrivial extraction of factorization-relevant information from an
instance without factoring it.

The mod-exponential sequence is the natural candidate substrate. The $p-1$ method
computes one deep term, $a^M \bmod N$; the sequence's early terms are essentially free.
This paper asks whether they say anything, and answers exhaustively.

\subsection{The empirical null}

A controlled experiment framed the question sharply. Thirty-six matched pairs were
generated with the bit-lengths of $p$ and $q$ pinned at $(18,20)$, differing only in the
smoothness of $p-1$: the SMOOTH arm used $p$ with $p-1$ built from primes below the
bound, the GENERAL arm used unconstrained $p$, both arms using unconstrained $q$. At
bound $B=100$ the $p-1$ method factored $35/36$ SMOOTH instances and $0/36$ GENERAL
instances --- the classes are operationally night and day.

Against that, $42$ windowed features were extracted at window length $m=256$ over bases
$\{2,3,5\}$, on both $s_x = a^x \bmod N$ and a floor-scaled twin: distinct-value count,
self-collision gap, top-bit balance, adjacent-difference statistics, autocorrelation,
spectral flatness, maximum run length. The largest observed standardized between-class
difference was $0.473$, against a permutation null with mean $0.495$ and $95$th
percentile $0.734$, giving $p = 0.502$. Five-fold cross-validated logistic regression
attained $\AUC = 0.500$.

Such a result is normally the end of a line of enquiry. Here the exactness of the figure
($0.500$) suggested a structural cause, and we found one.

